\section{Introduction}
Ranking tasks are fundamental to numerous real-world applications, such as search engines, recommendation systems, and document classification. In these contexts, the objective is to order a set of items based on specific user preferences. However, acquiring high-quality labels for ranking tasks is often difficult and costly, leading practitioners to rely on noisy labels generated through crowdsourcing or other approximate methods. Consequently, the field of \textit{noisy label ranking} has gained significant traction, with recent research focusing on developing robust algorithms to mitigate label noise.

This paper examines two recently introduced methods for noisy label ranking: \textit{NeR} (Niels et al., 2022) and \textit{NLE} (Zhang et al., 2022). Both approaches demonstrate promising empirical performance. \textit{NeR} employs a semi-supervised learning framework where initial training occurs on noisy data, followed by a label correction phase. In contrast, \textit{NLE} utilizes a specific estimator for the noisy data distribution to iteratively refine labels throughout the training process.

This work provides a theoretical analysis of the \textit{NeR} approach, which had previously lacked such guarantees. We demonstrate that the \textit{NeR} method achieves a convergence rate of $\mathcal{O}(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}})$, where $n$ represents the sample size. Additionally, we prove that \textit{NeR} is more general than earlier interpretations, as it can be effectively combined with diverse learning strategies, including supervised learning, active learning, and data cleaning.

