\section*{Introduction}

Abstract Interpretation \cite{Cousot74} is a static analysis technique that approximates a program's concrete fixpoint computation within an abstract domain. This approximation enables the identification of potential bugs that would otherwise require concrete execution to uncover. A significant advancement in this field is the integration of Abstract Interpretation with Symbolic Execution \cite{Godefroid03}, which enhances precision by utilizing path constraints to eliminate irrelevant program states during the abstract fixpoint calculation.

This paper presents a new abstract domain for function approximation capable of tightly approximating program behavior. Unlike many existing domains, this method offers a precision metric tailored to individual programs. We demonstrate that the domain can achieve arbitrary precision for a given program. We evaluate this domain on two database management systems, \texttt{SQLite} and \texttt{MySQL}, and report the detection of multiple bugs invisible to other static analyses.

\section*{Related Work}

The abstract domain for function approximation we propose is inspired by the concept of \textit{call graphs}. Call graphs represent the control flow of a program by mapping function calls to their definitions. In the context of Abstract Interpretation, this corresponds to a fixpoint computation that approximates every function call by linking the caller to the callee.

\section*{Conclusion}

This work has presented a novel abstract domain for function approximation, offering arbitrary precision for specific programs. The abstract domain is implemented in a tool that has been successfully applied to detect bugs in real-world database applications.

