Training agents to perform new tasks based on natural language instructions is a fundamental capability for building versatile systems. However, existing methods for instruction learning generally require large volumes of task-specific training data. This paper presents a framework designed to learn from a single instruction within a specific domain. Our approach involves two key components: (1) decomposing the learning process into intent recognition and intent generation, and (2) utilizing a text-based intermediate representation for the intent. We empirically validate the effectiveness of this framework across two distinct tasks: semantic parsing and essay writing. Our results achieve substantial improvements when learning from a single instruction compared to baseline approaches. Additionally, we provide evidence that this method allows agents to generalize to novel instructions during inference.
